    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a>, at Tell Mardikh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a>, was the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> kingdom in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>.</li><li>Its palace archive of about 17,000 tablets and fragments records a Semitic-speaking state of the 24th century BCE.</li><li>Destroyed around 2300 and again around 2000 BCE, it revived under Amorite kings until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hittite-empire">Hittites</a> destroyed it c. 1600 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The fire that destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a>’s palace around 2300 BCE baked its clay tablets hard, preserving one of the largest archives of the third millennium BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

