    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ibbit-lim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibbit-lim.jpg" alt="Ibbit-Lim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ibbit-lim">Ibbit-Lim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibbit-Lim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attar-Aram syria, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a> lay at Tell Mardikh, about 55 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>, on the rolling plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orontes-river">Orontes</a> valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euphrates">Euphrates</a>. A settlement existed there by about 3000 BCE, and during the first half of the third millennium it grew into a walled city with an acropolis and a lower town, part of a wider wave of urbanisation across Syria in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. By the twenty-fourth century BCE it was the capital of a kingdom that controlled many villages and towns, dealing on equal terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mari">Mari</a> on the Euphrates and Nagar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khabur-river">Khabur</a> region, and in contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kish">Kish</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>. Ebla’s ancient name was known from Mesopotamian inscriptions long before its location was found. An Italian mission from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sapienza-university-of-rome">University of Rome</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paolo-matthiae">Paolo Matthiae</a> began excavating in 1964, and in 1968 a statue dedicated by a king named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibbit-lim">Ibbit-Lim</a>, whose inscription mentioned Ebla, identified the site. In 1974 and 1975 the excavators found the archives of Palace G, the royal palace of the twenty-fourth century, which transformed knowledge of early Syrian history by showing that a literate, centralised state had flourished there alongside the city-states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumer">Sumer</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ebla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ebla.jpg" alt="Ebla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HPIM3078_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mappo, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Palace G archive, often counted at about 17,000 tablets and fragments, consists mostly of economic and administrative records from a period of perhaps forty or fifty years before the palace burned. It shows an economy based on cereals, olives and grapes and, above all, on vast flocks of sheep whose wool fed a large textile industry run by the palace. Cloth was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a>’s main product, issued to officials and envoys, given as gifts to other courts and exchanged for metals. The archives record large quantities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a>, and unworked lapis lazuli from distant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was found in the palace, as were fragments of Egyptian stone vessels inscribed with the names of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharaoh">pharaohs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khafre">Khafre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pepi-i">Pepi I</a>. The tablets were written in the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> script, used both for Sumerian words and for the local Semitic language, Eblaite, which is usually classed with Akkadian as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-semitic-languages">East Semitic</a>. The archive also contains literary texts, incantations and lexical lists, including bilingual Sumerian–Eblaite word lists among the oldest known dictionaries. Religious texts record offerings to many gods, among them Kura, the chief god of the city, the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hadad">Hadda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#resheph">Rasap</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ishkhara">Ishkhara</a>. In the second millennium Ebla’s Amorite population worshipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inanna">Ishtar</a> in a great temple.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-of-akkad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad.jpg" alt="Sargon of Akkad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a>’s rise brought it into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mari">Mari</a>, its chief rival for control of the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Texts from the archive, including a long letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#enna-dagan">Enna-Dagan</a> of Mari, record campaigns in which Mari’s kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iblul-il">Iblul-Il</a>, conquered towns in Ebla’s sphere, and for a time Ebla delivered large payments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> and gold to Mari. In the last decades of the archive Ebla turned the tables: in alliance with Nagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kish">Kish</a> it defeated Mari in a battle apparently fought near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#terqa">Terqa</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#euphrates">Euphrates</a>, an event recorded in the tablets. Ebla’s armies were raised from its towns and villages and led by the king and senior officials, but the texts say little about their organisation. The palace was destroyed by fire around 2300 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> listed Ebla among lands given to him by the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dagan">Dagan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> later boasted of conquering Ebla, so either may have been responsible, though some scholars argue that Mari took revenge first. In the second millennium the rebuilt city was protected by massive earthen ramparts with stone-built gates, enclosing an area of about 56 hectares. These defences could not save it from a Hittite attack around 1600 BCE, probably by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mursili-i">Mursili I</a> or his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#igrish-halam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igrish-halam.jpg" alt="Igrish-Halam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#igrish-halam">Igrish-Halam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Eblaite_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sémhur, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the archive period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a> was ruled by a king, called <em>malikum</em> in Eblaite, who presided over the palace, the main economic institution of the state. The names of several kings are known, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#igrish-halam">Igrish-Halam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#irkab-damu">Irkab-Damu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ishar-damu">Ishar-Damu</a>, the last king before the palace burned. Alongside the king stood a group of elders and a senior official often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizier</a>. Three successive holders of this office, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arrukum">Arrukum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibrium">Ibrium</a> and Ibrium’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibbi-zikir">Ibbi-Zikir</a>, appear so prominently in the tablets that they seem at times to have directed the administration and led military campaigns. The queen and the king’s mother also held important positions. Provincial towns were governed by local lords under the palace’s authority, and many smaller centres paid dues in goods or labour. The archives include what is probably one of the oldest known international treaties, between Ebla and a state called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abarsal">Abarsal</a>, setting out rules on boundaries, trade and the treatment of envoys and fugitives. Ebla exchanged messengers and gifts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mari">Mari</a>, Nagar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kish">Kish</a> and other courts, a diplomatic system centuries older than the better-known one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akhetaten">Amarna</a> age. In the second millennium Ebla was ruled by Amorite kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibbit-lim">Ibbit-Lim</a>, and by the eighteenth century it had become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> of the Amorite kingdom of Yamhad, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sodom-and-gomorrah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sodom-and-gomorrah.jpg" alt="Sodom and Gomorrah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sodom-and-gomorrah">Sodom and Gomorrah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Martin_-_Sodom_and_Gomorrah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Martin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a>’s discovery changed the picture of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a>. Before 1974, historians saw third-millennium <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> largely as a periphery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>; the archive showed a literate state with its own language, gods, institutions and diplomacy, which had adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> writing and Sumerian scholarly traditions and adapted them to its own needs. The tablets provide the oldest substantial body of texts in a Semitic language other than Akkadian, and they illuminate the trade in textiles and metals that linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>, Mesopotamia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. Ebla’s bilingual word lists have helped scholars understand both Sumerian and early Semitic vocabulary. The early publicity surrounding the find also produced controversy: claims that the tablets mentioned biblical cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sodom-and-gomorrah">Sodom and Gomorrah</a> or biblical figures were widely reported in the 1970s but have since been rejected by most specialists. The second-millennium city, with its ramparts, palaces, temples and royal tombs, is a key site for understanding Amorite urban culture in Syria, and finds in the royal tombs, including a ceremonial mace bearing the name of an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Thirteenth Dynasty, show continuing contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>. Work at the site was halted by the Syrian civil war in 2011.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#aleppo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleppo.jpg" alt="Aleppo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleppo_Citadel_entrance_-_seen_from_southeast_9933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a> was destroyed and rebuilt more than once before it finally disappeared. The burning of Palace G around 2300 BCE ended the great kingdom of the archive period, whether the attacker was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akkad">Akkad</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mari">Mari</a>. The fire baked the clay tablets and preserved them, which is why the archive survived. The city was soon reoccupied, and a second Ebla existed in the last centuries of the third millennium; texts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> mention messengers from Ebla, but this kingdom left far fewer records and was itself destroyed around 2000 BCE. Amorite settlers then rebuilt the city on a larger scale, with new ramparts, palaces and temples, and this third Ebla prospered in the early second millennium. However, it never regained its former independence and leadership: by the eighteenth century BCE it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> of Yamhad, whose kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a> dominated northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>. When the Hittite kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> campaigned against Yamhad in the late seventeenth and early sixteenth centuries BCE, Ebla was destroyed, around 1600 BCE. A Hurrian–Hittite poem known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-of-release">Song of Release</a> appears to describe its fall. Only small villages occupied the mound afterwards, and Ebla dropped out of history until its rediscovery in the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ebla">Ebla</a> was one of the earliest literate states outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>; the archive of its burned palace revealed a Semitic-speaking kingdom of the twenty-fourth century BCE with its own textile economy, treaties and diplomacy, and overturned the idea that early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> was merely a periphery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumer">Sumer</a>.</em></p>

